A company depends on a single upstream supplier that continously increses material prices,destorying the company's manufacturing margins
                                             Abstract
Strategic Cost Management (SCM) extends conventional cost management by examining the strategic, operational, and external factors that influence an organization's cost structure.

In manufacturing environments, material procurement is an important component of overall production cost and can directly influence product profitability.

Supplier relationships form a critical part of the manufacturing value chain because organizations depend on external suppliers for raw materials, components, and other production requirements.

Excessive dependence on a single supplier can reduce an organization's purchasing flexibility and increase exposure to unfavorable changes in supplier pricing.

Continuous increases in the price of a critical material can raise procurement expenditure and subsequently increase the manufacturing cost of the finished product.

When increased manufacturing costs cannot be transferred to customers through corresponding changes in selling prices, the additional cost places direct pressure on manufacturing margins.

The problem is therefore not limited to procurement cost alone; it involves supplier concentration, bargaining power, cost exposure, operational dependency, and financial performance.

This project examines the supplier price dependency problem using concepts from Strategic Cost Management, supplier dependency analysis, cost-driver analysis, and value-chain analysis.

The project aims to translate the identified real-world problem into a practical analytical prototype capable of examining supplier concentration, material-price changes, cost impact, and margin sensitivity under different scenarios.

The development will follow an iterative engineering approach in which assumptions, implementation decisions, errors, risks, limitations, experimental findings, and improvements are documented throughout the development process.
1. Introduction
Manufacturing organizations operate within interconnected supply, production, and distribution networks where decisions at one stage can influence costs at subsequent stages.

Raw materials and externally sourced components can represent a significant portion of the total manufacturing cost.

Procurement decisions therefore influence not only purchasing expenditure but also production economics and product profitability.

Traditional cost management commonly focuses on budgeting, expenditure monitoring, variance analysis, and corrective financial control.

Such approaches can identify that costs have increased, but may not sufficiently explain the structural or external factors responsible for the increase.

Supplier relationships are an important external factor because organizations frequently depend on suppliers for materials, components, specialized processes, and production-critical resources.

Dependence on a single supplier can provide operational advantages such as established quality standards, predictable processes, volume purchasing, and simplified supplier management.

At the same time, excessive dependence can reduce the organization's ability to respond when the supplier changes prices, reduces capacity, or introduces unfavorable commercial conditions.

A continuous increase in the price of a critical material can propagate through the manufacturing system, increasing procurement expenditure and production costs.

When the resulting increase in manufacturing cost cannot be transferred to customers through higher selling prices, the additional cost directly places pressure on the organization's margin.

The issue therefore extends beyond procurement and becomes a strategic cost-management problem involving financial exposure, supplier concentration, operational flexibility, and long-term competitiveness.

2. Theoretical Foundation
2.1 Strategic Cost Management
Strategic Cost Management (SCM) examines cost in relation to the broader strategic objectives of an organization.

It extends beyond the recording of historical expenditure by considering the factors that create and influence cost.

Cost management can therefore involve decisions related to suppliers, production processes, technology, capacity, organizational structure, and product characteristics.

SCM considers both internal and external influences on an organization's cost position.

The approach emphasizes understanding the causes of cost rather than treating total expenditure as an isolated financial value.

Strategic cost decisions should consider their long-term consequences rather than focusing exclusively on immediate savings.

Cost reduction should be evaluated alongside requirements such as quality, reliability, delivery, safety, and operational continuity.

Supplier dependency can be treated as a structural source of cost exposure within this framework.

Scenario analysis can be used to examine how changes in external conditions may affect organizational costs.

In this project, SCM provides the conceptual foundation for analyzing supplier price dependency and its impact on manufacturing margins.

2.2 Supplier Dependency and Concentration
Supplier dependency refers to the degree to which an organization relies on one or a limited number of suppliers for required materials or components.

Dependency becomes more significant when the supplied material is critical to production.

High concentration can reduce the organization's flexibility when commercial conditions change.

Limited alternative suppliers can increase the bargaining power of the primary supplier.

Switching suppliers may require qualification, testing, contractual changes, tooling changes, or additional logistics arrangements.

A supplier offering a low purchase price may still introduce risks through lead time, quality, reliability, or capacity limitations.

Supplier concentration can therefore represent both a cost exposure and an operational risk.

The financial impact of dependency depends on factors such as purchase volume, material price, supplier allocation, and product cost structure.

Diversification can reduce dependency but may introduce additional procurement or operational costs.

Supplier decisions should therefore be evaluated using multiple cost and risk factors rather than purchase price alone.

2.3 Cost Driver Analysis
Cost Driver Analysis focuses on the factors responsible for changes in cost.

Material purchase price is a direct cost driver in the supplier-price problem.

Material consumption determines how strongly a price change affects total procurement expenditure.

Supplier allocation determines the organization's exposure to a particular supplier's pricing decisions.

Production volume affects the total quantity of material required.

Transportation and logistics can contribute additional costs when alternative suppliers are geographically distant.

Lead time can influence inventory requirements and production planning.

Quality and rejection rates can create additional material and processing costs.

Supplier capacity can affect availability and procurement flexibility.

Contractual conditions can influence the organization's ability to negotiate or respond to future price changes.

2.4 Value Chain Perspective
Value Chain Analysis examines the interconnected activities involved in creating and delivering a product.

Supplier selection represents an important upstream activity.

Procurement determines how materials are acquired and under what commercial conditions.

Transportation and receiving introduce additional cost and time considerations.

Inventory management influences holding costs and material availability.

Production converts purchased materials into finished or semi-finished products.

Quality control determines whether purchased and processed materials meet required specifications.

Assembly and downstream operations depend on the availability and quality of required inputs.

Distribution and delivery connect manufacturing output with the final customer.

A supplier price increase can therefore propagate through multiple stages of the value chain rather than remaining an isolated procurement expense.

2.5 Manufacturing Margin
Manufacturing cost represents the expenditure associated with producing a product.

Material cost is one of the major components that can influence manufacturing cost.

A change in the price of a production-critical material can therefore affect the economics of each manufactured unit.

A simplified relationship can be represented as:

Manufacturing Margin = Selling Price − Manufacturing Cost

When material prices increase while the selling price remains unchanged, manufacturing cost increases.

An increase in manufacturing cost consequently reduces the available margin.

The magnitude of the impact depends on the material's contribution to the overall manufacturing cost.

Products with high material intensity may experience greater margin sensitivity to supplier price changes.

Repeated price increases can create cumulative financial pressure if selling prices cannot be adjusted proportionately.

This relationship forms the basis for the scenario and margin analysis that will be developed in the prototype.
